Date: Sun, 14 Mar 1993 21:17:36 -0800 (PST)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: BRAIN: Creatures of infinite habit

Paul Cisek:
>   Aside
>   from the obvious issues of overpopulation (do we let immortals
>   have children?) 

Who is "we"?  How do "we" obtain the power to "let" immortals
have children?

Heinlein as Lazarus Long noted that the word "obvious" is a
reliable channel marker, indicating something that need not
be so.  This is a great example.  "Overpopulation" is a value 
judgement, not an objective condition.  Given my extropian values, 
demographics make it "obvious" that underpopulation is a serious 
problem in many cultures, and might also be so in the era of uploaded 
immortality.  (See my previous voluminous posts on the subject...)

>   For one, an immortal is susceptible to becoming nothing
>   more than a creature of habit, without capacity for adaptation
>   to a world that continues to change...

This characteristic, along with lack of motivation & fit values, will 
tend to be selected out in an upload environment.  The winners
could be the a-life already adapted to the environment,
unless we learn some of their tricks.  Even without a-life,
those uploaders who adapt the best will dominate the resource space.
Going from hunter-gatherer bodies to software means radical adaption; 
see my previous posts for comments on sexual engineering etc.

> 	   ...various brain systems
> 	   develop from an initial state of plasticity toward a final
> 	   state of stability. 

This is a phenomenon of the current, unaugmented brain.  The
effect is not absolute; eg George Bernard Shaw continued writing original
plays into his 90's.  Several technologies will likely make it possible
to revert the brain to infantile plasticity before we upload.
Candidates include nerve growth factors and neural tissue transplant
(two very active fields of research in search of cures for 
Alzheimer's and Parkinson's), and brain implants including 
neural nets, heads-up computers, cellphones, etc.  This tech is based
more on trial-and-error than deep understanding of the
brain, so I think they fit your assumptions. There may also be
psychological methods of refreshing the brain.  

For these methods to help, one must have the resources and extropian 
motivations to use them, and perhaps for many such motivation may
be a problem.  

I don't look at this self-transformation as mere regression to previous
states or ages; I won't lose my years of experience in the process
of, say, implanting part of the language-learning portion of my
brain with fetal neurons. (At least most neurologists think memory
is redundant enough that the brain can adapt to such implants;
transplant experiments with Alzheimers patients should resolve many of
these issues).  I look forward to continuous improvement with 
new technology as it becomes available, but probably won't change
too radically all at once.

> 	   What is the ideal set of
> 	   plasticity/stability tradeoffs desirable? 
> 	   Without stability is learning possible?

Learning requires some instability.  These are very good questions,
though.  Perhaps different parts of the brain can be age 1 (for learning
motor skills), age 4 (for learning language), age 100 (the conservative
geezer agent), etc.  Thus, I might have new motor skill neuron
transplants annually, language learning transplants every four years,
etc.  After uploading I'll perhaps choose from the best learning
algorithms available from captured a-life, as well as neural
simulations.

