Subject: Enneagram (was Is writing more satisfying than conversation?)

Previously I wrote:
>[. . . My personality type
>(Myers-Briggs INTJ, Enneagram 5w6,->translate to your favorite
>personality map)[...]

To which Thomas Burns asked:
>Where does " Enneagram 5w6" come from?


The 5w6 refers to Enneagram personality type 5 with a type 6 wing. 
The Enneagram is simply another personality type system. I've noticed
that many on this list subscribe to the Myers Briggs personality type
system, which is fine if it works for you. I found that it didn't work 
so well for me and for the people that I know. After taking the test and
learning that my type was strongly polarized on the INTJ axes, I read
some of the literature on what this type implies and I thought that there
were some big gaps in how it described my personality.  Similar things 
happened to a couple of my friends. I thought that the Enneagram described
my personality type alot better, and I use it frequently as a tool to try
to understand the people around me. I can interact with other people far better
once I get a handle on what their world-view (i.e. personality type) is like.
I also understand my own motivations better and I use this system to 
change my thinking patterns, especially if I'm stuck in a loop about something.

Briefly the Enneagram is this:
A personality system that divides all the range of types into 9 types:
Type One: The Reformer
Type Two: The Helper
Type Three: The Status Seeker
Type Four: The Artist
Type Five: The Thinker
Type Six: The Loyalist
Type Seven: The Generalist
Type Eight: The Leader
Type Nine: The Peacemaker

An organizing principle uses triads to break these 9 types up into 3
triads: The Feeling Triad, the Doing Triad, and the Relating Triad. 
These triads specify your fundamental psychological orientation, which
includes positive and negative traits.

Whereas the Myers-Briggs system has its roots in Jungian psychology,
the Enneagram has its roots in sufism and Gurdjieff. 

I'll stop here because it's a rather detailed system, and if you're
interested, you can learn more about it on your own. There are many
books about it in the "self-help" section in your bookstore. The book
that I use as a reference is: _Personality Types_ by Don Riso.  


