X-Reposting-Policy: redistribute only with permission

>From: pavel@park.bu.edu (Paul Cisek)

>  Immortality may not be all that it's cracked up to be.  Aside
>  from the obvious issues of overpopulation (do we let immortals
>  have children?) there are potential problems at an individual
>  level.  For one, an immortal is susceptible to becoming nothing
>  more than a creature of habit, without capacity for adaptation
>  to a world that continues to change...

Certainly a possible problem.

>  Let's consider the life of an immortal human given the following
>  assumptions (for the sake of argument only):

>  1)   Uploading comes before an understanding of "mind" which
>	   comes before the ability to radically redefine it.

>  2)   Uploading is done using a "brute-force" approach such as
>	   the simulation of all pertinent neural dynamics normally
>	   implemented biologically.  This allows brain backup and
>	   copying which effectively makes the "person" immortal.

>  3)   We ignore (for the moment) the deep philosophical issue of
>	   "what is consciousness?" and concentrate strictly on the
>	   behavioral aspects of the uploaded mind.

I'll note that even though when you upload no theory of the mind might
be developed, that doesn't mean that none will ever be developed.
Presumably you can wait quite a while for the research to be done...

>  4)   Current psychological theories of development are not
>	   completely false.  To summarize, various brain systems
>	   develop from an initial state of plasticity toward a final
>	   state of stability.  This development is often
>	   characterized by "critical periods" or times during which
>	   the functionality of a system is determined (for the large
>	   part, due to environmental input).  For humans the bulk of
>	   development takes place before birth, but though most
>	   critical periods occur before the age of twenty, the brain
>	   continues its progress towards stability all throughout
>	   life.  Let's speculate that this "hardening" of brain
>	   function is a basic neural principle, as it indeed seems to
>	   be.

>  I'm interested in what choices people would make, or what other
>  solutions they can suggest.  I ask you to refrain from
>  suggestions that we simply "redesign the mind".  For the sake of
>  argument, let's suppose that this is impossible.

I find it very hard to believe that it will never be possible. Never
is a very long time, especially for immortals.

>  I believe that
>  there is no absolute mechanism for thought, and that instead
>  what we experience and call thinking and living is a direct
>  result of how our brain specifically works.  Given that it was
>  designed under evolutionary motivations we must assume that the
>  dynamics that govern its behavior have no concern for longevity.
>  Assuming also that we want to remain as qualitatively human
>  minds, we must prepare to deal with this handicap.

>Comments?

I, for one, am not extremely worried. This seems like the sort of
engineering problem we will start worrying about in the earliest parts
of the fall into the singularity -- between now and then, there are
lots of other things to worry about that aren't speculative, like
surviving until then at all. I suspect that our current knowledge of
minds is insufficient to even begin to discuss this problem in a
realistic way -- its much like people who've just discovered fire
trying to have a rational discussion about blast furnace design. We
really know very little about the mind that we can be sure of at this
point other than a few basics like that it seems to be rooted in its
physical substrate. When we know enough to discuss it intelligently,
I'll start worrying.

However, just for sake of conversation, I'll note that it is my hope
to redesign my mind in the future (while, of course, it still
operates) in order to allow me to learn and reason better than I can
now. Given that this is a goal of mine, becoming "set in my ways" is
something I hope to avoid.

Perry

